WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.769
PAULSON: Good evening, welcome. Thank you
very much for coming out on this icy evening.

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:32.029
I'm delighted to be here in Washington.
For those of you who don't know, following

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:39.940
my time as treasury secretary, I returned
to my hometown of Chicago and I spend my time

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:49.320
with the Paulson Institute -- an NGO that's
focused on U.S.-China relations. So, that's

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:56.360
-- that's really how I spend my time.
It's a think-and-do tank; it's focused on

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:02.180
strengthening the relationship between the
U.S. and China with an emphasis on economic

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.680
growth -- sustainable economic growth in the
environment and the intersection of those

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.810
two.
Now, although we are active in Washington

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:18.159
and we have several of our very important
people here, that I think the last time we

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:26.450
sponsored an event in Washington was 2011.
So, we're just very honored to be cosponsoring

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.050
this with the Council on Foreign Relations,
to be here today.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:38.329
And we have a panel of stellar speakers so
I'm going to be quite brief here because we've

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:43.130
got some -- I think some terrific experts
on China you'll be hearing from in a minute.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:49.860
But just a few words at the outset, which
I think most of you know, that is the world's

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:57.250
two largest economies, the United States and
China, play a leading role in powering growth,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:02.500
rebalancing the global economy and ultimately
tackling some of the biggest challenges we

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.300
have in the world.
And, you know, we've got some huge ones; I

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.979
happen to think that climate change is one
-- one of the -- one of the very biggest ones.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:17.040
But in today's world, when you think about
almost any of the major challenges, they're

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.810
all easier to solve if our two countries are
working together in a complementary way and

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.130
they're much, much more difficult if we're
not.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:33.450
And, you know, on the economic front -- and
that to me is a real fabric of the investment

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:39.810
-- excuse me, the relationship; that's really
-- that is where we have the most important

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.540
bridge.
And here I think it is very important that

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:52.150
we continue to work to strengthen that and
-- and we have -- I'll just say a couple words

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:58.760
about the bilateral investment treaty because
I just think that's very, very important and

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.209
it's -- it can be a key lever in opening up
Chinese markets for U.S. companies and driving

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:10.370
reform and to the extent we get more Chinese
investment here and the right kind of investment,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:20.489
it's going to create -- create jobs.
I can think of no threat that poses a greater

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:27.459
risk to the environment or just in -- generally
an economic risk than, as I said earlier,

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:33.420
climate change. And I think the key to solving
this is having the U.S. and China work together

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.670
in complementary ways and -- and frankly,
I don't know how we're -- if we have any chance

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:43.830
of solving it if we're not. And so, therefore,
I was very, very encouraged by the breakthrough

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:52.590
that -- that our two governments had at APEC
and I think that's a terrific start on that.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:04:01.010
I'll just say one word -- the Paulson Institute
has a -- a number of programs; we have an

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:09.769
air quality and climate program; we've put
together a U.S.-China CEO Urbanization Council

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:15.409
to work on these challenges of energy and
we've got eight outstanding CEOs on each side

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:23.300
and one of those, David Cote of Honeywell,
is -- is here today.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:31.000
We've got some -- our think tank is putting
out, I think, some, you know, some papers

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.960
that are very important on dealing with some
of the fundamental challenges in the energy

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:42.710
and policy area, and across investment.
So, the reason I say this is I think one of

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:52.220
the things it's going to take as we, you know,
work on these major challenges, it takes governments,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:59.180
it takes the corporate sector and it takes
NGOs all working together -- and -- and hence

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:04.090
the -- the panel tonight.
So, having said that, I'm going to turn it

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:12.030
over to Liz Economy. And as you all know,
she is a real China expert; she's a senior

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:20.830
fellow here. I remember, although as depressing
as it was, it was right, reading her first

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:28.080
book, The River Runs Black. She's -- she's
written another very important book and so,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:41.810
I'm, as all of you are, looking forward to
listening to this panel moderated by Liz.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:59.340
So, over to you, Liz.
(APPLAUSE)

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:06.300
ECONOMY: Thank you, Hank, for that lovely
introduction. And let me thank you, as well,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:11.850
for all the work that you did as treasury
secretary in -- groundbreaking work, really

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:18.410
-- in establishing the foundation for the
U.S.-China economic and environmental relationships.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:23.069
And I know that everyone here is looking forward
to hearing more about your work and about

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:28.410
your thoughts when your new book dealing with
China is released in April.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:33.889
Now, on behalf of the Council on Foreign Relations
and the Paulson Institute, let me welcome

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:39.550
everybody here. We really are privileged to
have such a distinguished panel of thinkers

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:45.860
and doers to discuss this evening's topic,
the China Challenge: Balancing Cooperation

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.979
and Competition.
I'm just going to briefly introduce all of

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:55.729
the speakers. You have their full bios before
you and, frankly, if I were to take the time

00:06:55.729 --> 00:07:00.820
to do the full bios, it would take up all
the time that we have this evening.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.780
And then I'm just going to ask each of them
a question or two to begin the conversation

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:08.560
before opening the floor for questions from
all of you.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.680
Just a few housekeeping notes -- please, everybody,
turn off your phones, your beepers, anything

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:21.300
that may -- might make some noise. This meeting
is on the record, so not traditional Council

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:30.220
style, and we will end promptly at 7:15.
So, without further ado, let me introduce

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:37.550
our speakers. To my immediate right, of course,
is Madeleine Albright. Secretary Albright

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:42.610
is chair of Albright Stonebridge Group, a
global strategy firm, and chair of Albright

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:47.770
Capital Management LLC, an investment advisory
firm that's focused on emerging markets.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:53.610
She was, of course, most notably the 64th
secretary of state of the United States and

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:59.289
she just returned from Beijing where she participated
in a dialogue between the Aspen Strategy Group

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:03.699
and the Central Party School in China. So,
I know we're going to have some fresh insights

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:09.789
from you on that.
To Secretary Albright's right is Dave Cote.

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:16.979
Mr. Cote was named chairman and CEO of Honeywell
on July 1st, 2002. Under his leadership, the

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:22.569
company has grown from $22 billion in sales
into a highly profitable technology leader

00:08:22.569 --> 00:08:29.770
with more than $40 billion in sales today.
Mr. Cote was unsurprisingly named 2013's CEO

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:37.150
of the Year by Chief Executive magazine.
To my left, I have Jon Huntsman. Governor

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:42.789
Huntsman began his career in public service
as staff assistant to President Ronald Reagan.

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:48.330
He has served each of the four U.S. presidents
since then in critical roles around the world,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:52.810
including as ambassador to Singapore, deputy
assistant secretary of commerce for Asia,

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:57.399
U.S. trade ambassador and, most recently,
U.S. ambassador to China.

00:08:57.399 --> 00:09:02.630
He's currently chairman of the Atlantic Council
Board of Directors and co-chair for No Labels.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:09.740
And last but certainly not least, Jin-Yong
Cai. Dr. Cai joined IFC as executive vice

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:15.980
president and CEO in 2012 following two decades
in the financial services industry. His experience

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:20.180
includes twelve years with Goldman Sachs Group
where he was part of the firm's global leadership

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:22.980
team and he served as the top executive in
China.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:27.970
At IFC, which is a member of the World Bank
Group, Dr. Cai's work is focused on bringing

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:32.420
private sector solutions to the biggest development
challenges in emerging markets.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:39.420
So, let me begin by asking all of you the
same question and have you focus on the internal

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:42.529
dynamics of China and then we'll take a step
back and talk about the U.S.-China relationship.

00:09:42.529 --> 00:09:50.170
But as you look inside China today, how do
you assess Chinese President Xi Jinping's

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:54.000
first two years in office? What do you see
as the most sort of salient political and

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.839
economic features of a Xi-led China and what
do they signify for U.S. interests?

00:09:58.839 --> 00:10:05.530
I'll give each of you about four minutes or
so to address this issue and then we'll move

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:09.500
on from there. So, let me start with you,
Governor Huntsman, and ask you to set the

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:14.760
stage for us.
HUNTSMAN: Well, it's -- it's a -- it's a big

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.490
question. And let me just begin by -- by saying
this -- you know, I'm a practitioner; I see

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:24.370
a lot of experts in the audience. We're going
to put this relationship to the test, not

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:30.860
necessarily during the years between elections
but, indeed, as we approach 2016.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:36.610
So, whatever we're experiencing now is likely
to experience a little bit of tumult as we

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:41.200
approach the presidential election, which
is going to put Xi Jinping to the test, along

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.519
with his new team. And it's certainly going
to test our side of the system, as well.

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:50.769
I would say that the leadership style that
I'm seeing out of Xi Jinping is rather unprecedented.

00:10:50.769 --> 00:10:56.240
I'm not sure that there was any analyst or
expert who necessarily predicted the extent

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:01.440
to which he would consolidate power, the extent
to which he would lead out in a fairly unilateral

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:07.839
and aggressive and assertive fashion.
I think it all kind of is somewhat reflective

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:13.380
of the leadership style more under Mao Zedong
or Deng Xiaoping as compared to anything that

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:19.460
we've seen most recently. I think he's serious
about consolidating power. He's not done yet.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:27.100
And I think we're likely to see the anti-corruption
campaign pivot from retired senior cadre like

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:33.950
Zhou Yongkang and others to currently-serving
cadre and I think that's going to be a new

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:39.070
and -- and -- and challenging phase for the
system.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:45.300
I also think his decisionmaking, which is
far different than we saw under Hu Jintao

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:49.520
who was very much by consensus as opposed
to the unilateral approach that Xi Jinping

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:57.540
takes -- I think it's -- it's -- it's facilitated
and aided by a standing committee, which is

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:01.290
different than the standing committee under
Hu Jintao. It's smaller; I think they're more

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:06.260
likeminded. I think he's got some pragmatic
former provincial governors who are really

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:12.450
intent on economic reforms that are absolutely
critical to China's survival going forward.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:16.950
But what I think I'm most intrigued by, other
than all of the economic reforms that have

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:22.430
been talked about -- teed up at the 18th party
and then built upon at the Third Plenum -- is

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:26.579
a little bit of what we're hearing out of
the Fourth Plenum most recently, which was

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:31.720
on legal and political reform.
So, you know, as we've seen a lot of backsliding

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:39.649
and -- and behavior that has been I think
unseen since 1989, we're beginning to hear

00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:45.380
a little bit more about (inaudible), you know,
a country based on rule of law.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:50.029
I'm not sure that anyone quite knows what
that means but I think there's some sense

00:12:50.029 --> 00:13:00.279
of optimism, at least as I see it, that there
is resistance to the old class struggle campaign

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:05.350
and an attempt more to move toward economic
development and a new approach to rule of

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:13.490
law that first will be applied to economics
and trade and inevitably move toward civil

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.190
society. I don't know how -- how it won't;
that will take some years to do.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:22.180
But I think there -- there is a push on the
part of Xi Jinping and some of his likeminded

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:29.380
colleagues to begin to start that debate on
rule of law and -- and even the constitutional

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:34.010
implications of what that means. And I think
we're beginning a very interesting phase here

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:38.600
that again, like the anti-corruption campaign,
is going to be very difficult for the China

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.060
watchers to necessarily predict where we're
going.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:46.410
And then we'll hit the 19th party Congress
and you can already feel it in the air -- the

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:52.230
personnel transitions. An example would be
Mayor Sun of Tianjin, also a member of the

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:59.029
Politburo, who was just put in Ling Jihua's
place as head of the United Front Work program.

00:13:59.029 --> 00:14:03.120
So, I think we'll hear more about personnel
issues that are being teed up for the 19th

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:08.410
party Congress. I think that will represent
the end of the consolidation of power in between

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:14.081
the 19th and the 20th party Congresses -- so,
2017 to 2022. I think we're likely to hear

00:14:14.081 --> 00:14:19.511
a lot more about reform efforts. And you'll
have -- she will have the solid base with

00:14:19.511 --> 00:14:23.010
which to go after the reforms in a fairly
aggressive way.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:28.470
So, I think the next -- the -- the period
between now and the -- and the 19th party

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:33.329
Congress is going to be very, very interesting
to see who the new personnel selections are

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:38.430
because that will have huge implications with
respect to then the final reform drive up

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:41.639
to the 20th party Congress.
ECONOMY: Great; that's a very positive and

00:14:41.639 --> 00:14:46.450
even exciting path forward I think that you've
laid out for China.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:51.140
Secretary Albright, does that resonate with
you in your recent visit to China? And I know

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:54.230
you've met with President Xi Jinping. How
do you see situation evolving?

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:57.579
ALBRIGHT: Well, yes and no, frankly -- and
I'm delighted to be here and also to be able

00:14:57.579 --> 00:15:02.080
to do something with Hank. Your institute
is terrific.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:07.510
I think that -- I -- I see a lot of what Jon
has said absolutely true in terms of the strength

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.610
of the leadership.
What was interesting -- the first time I met

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:16.519
with Xi Jinping, I'd gone over there in a
very peculiar meeting which was Democrats

00:15:16.519 --> 00:15:22.209
and Republicans meeting with the International
Department of the Chinese Communist Party.

00:15:22.209 --> 00:15:26.630
And we were frankly trying to figure out what
we were doing there and why they wanted the

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:32.750
dialogue and they did introduce us to Xi Jinping.
Then what happened was when he came to the

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:37.790
United States for his visit as vice premier
at the time, he had a bunch of his former

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:45.320
people over to talk for a dinner that he had
set up. And I -- it was interesting because

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:51.090
he was asking us questions in terms of what
we had learned over the three decades of our

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:55.350
relationship -- Hank, you were there -- you
know, in terms of what we had learned about

00:15:55.350 --> 00:16:00.930
our relationship. And it struck me as a very
kind of forthcoming, interesting way of trying

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:06.779
to bring us into the whole process.
What -- I have followed everything very closely

00:16:06.779 --> 00:16:12.230
and I -- what I see is very much kind of the
tough part that you were talking about, in

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:16.890
terms of consolidating the party.
The meeting that we had -- David was there

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:23.610
-- was one with the Central Party School.
There were a lot of people, all who had exactly

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:30.810
the same thing to say. And so, really kind
of an example of keeping the order together.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:35.959
I think that he knows that he is dealing with
corruption in the country and he is using

00:16:35.959 --> 00:16:41.920
that, I think, very cleverly to rid himself
of people that he doesn't want to deal with.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:47.079
I think the question on -- we had some private
discussions on the rule -- was it rule of

00:16:47.079 --> 00:16:53.410
law or rule by laws and what does -- which
way is it really going and is the translation

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:59.089
even correct and where -- he is providing
a tool for himself, I think, to be able to

00:16:59.089 --> 00:17:04.350
do something.
I think there is huge desire within the ruling

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:10.640
groups to have an outward relationship and
to put China in a position where we regard

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:16.939
it with respect. There's an awful lot about
do we use the right words in terms of our

00:17:16.939 --> 00:17:24.049
discussion with them; are we condescending
-- a number of kind of nuanced issues on that.

00:17:24.049 --> 00:17:27.390
But basically, China is here, China is in
power.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:33.600
What I found that was interesting was that
this is not particularly a happy time for

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:39.930
business in China because part of what is
going on are some laws that make it more and

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:44.240
more complicated.
So, the anti-monopoly law or issues to do

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:49.299
with cyber -- you can probably talk about
that more, David. But I think basically that

00:17:49.299 --> 00:17:55.130
makes it a little bit more complicated in
trying to figure out how things really work.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:18:00.880
And a desire to have American business or
foreign investment and especially -- I mean,

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:07.670
if one looks at the numbers of our trade relationship,
you know, it used to be somehow the equivalent

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:13.710
of Honduras and now it's $600 billion. So,
this is a very important relationship to them

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:17.780
and to us.
But some of the -- the laws and decrees that

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:23.250
are coming out I think make it complicated
and the regulations and which bureaucracy's

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:28.590
in charge -- which I think there's a little
bit to do with this sense of how do we obey

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:33.640
the new way of looking at things. So, as everything
that's ever been written about a bureaucracy

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.210
is true...
(LAUGHTER)

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:42.130
And so, there is this kind of sense -- how
to carry out the regulations and not delete

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:47.100
their own power and yet be able to follow
and do what the leader wants.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:52.340
I think he's very impressive; there's no question.
And I think that the possibility -- Hank,

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.360
you were talking about the agreement on climate
change. I think that's a really big deal -- that

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.890
he could do that is definitely a sign of the
power of somebody.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:06.480
And this is the important part. Here is a
man who sees himself in office for many, many

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:11.870
years -- who thinks he's going to see a lot
of different American presidents and that

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:17.240
he -- our foreign policy comes in four year
segments, his is going to come as a longer

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:22.580
time and that kind of sense of being there
for a long time I think is very important.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:28.330
What is interesting despite what I said is
that there is a desire to get American help

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:33.400
in a number of ways. And domestically, for
instance, I met with the health minister.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:38.070
She made it very clear that they have problems
in their health care system; they would like

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:43.380
to get some advice from the United States,
try to figure out how we can work together...

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:48.620
(LAUGHTER)
It depends on who you're for. But -- or for

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:54.059
instance, issues to do with food safety or
farming that there are a variety of issues

00:19:54.059 --> 00:19:58.320
on which we can help.
I do think where there is an issue, and we'll

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:03.850
talk more about U.S.-Chinese relations, there
is no question that as one looks at the history

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:09.169
of the U.S.-Chinese relationship, the business
community has been the greatest supporter

00:20:09.169 --> 00:20:12.860
of it.
And if, in fact, it becomes more complicated

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:17.919
to do business, then some of the issues to
do with human rights and a variety of other

00:20:17.919 --> 00:20:21.760
issues will make it more difficult on the
relationship.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:28.289
So, I find it fascinating to watch what he's
doing. I agree, in terms of he's much more

00:20:28.289 --> 00:20:34.169
like Deng Xiaoping or Mao. But here is a really,
really strong leader who sees himself in there

00:20:34.169 --> 00:20:42.059
for a very long time, who has the tools to
adjust the system in a way to refurbish the

00:20:42.059 --> 00:20:47.760
Communist Party and put it back central and
also to deal with people that he doesn't like.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:53.280
The question is -- and I always have this
question anywhere -- if you create a middle

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:58.330
class, which he has done, at what point do
they want to be part of the system and have

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:04.680
a voice and -- and -- and what happens to
a political system that's top-down when you

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.690
have created a middle class?
ECONOMY: I think we'll come back later and

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:11.690
have you address just that question, because
I think it is one of the most central questions

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:15.980
in China today.
So, let me use your excellent introduction

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:21.070
to the issues of business in China to ask
Mr. Cote to talk a little bit about how you

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:26.400
see the reform process underway in China today
-- what it means for Honeywell in particular

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:31.580
because I know you have your fingers in almost
every part of economic pie in China, from

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:36.370
infrastructure to energy to environmental
technology. So, what do you see coming out

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:40.770
of Xi's first two years?
COTE: Yeah, from my perspective, his first

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:45.039
two years have been impressive.
And just to put it into perspective, we now

00:21:45.039 --> 00:21:52.909
have 12,000 employees in China. And two years
ago, China became the biggest country outside

00:21:52.909 --> 00:21:58.230
the U.S. for our company sales. So, we've
made a pretty big commitment because I really

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:04.919
believe that China could be the economic story
of the 21st century.

00:22:04.919 --> 00:22:10.760
And the stuff that we've seen him doing has
been I think very good. The whole anti-corruption

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:16.740
push which we could say has a dark side to
it also -- at the end of the day, is unbelievably

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:23.539
needed and unbelievably supported by the population.
I mean, my own employees talk about the stories

00:22:23.539 --> 00:22:28.080
that they see in the press every day, in the
Chinese press, and how important they think

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:32.510
that is to see this happening.
The commitment on climate change I thought

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:36.550
was impressive. The population supports him
in it.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:40.600
The concern that I have about it is exactly
what Madeleine was talking about -- is once

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:45.809
somebody's accumulated and consolidated power,
can they transition? And if they can't transition,

00:22:45.809 --> 00:22:49.370
well, a lot of the things that we hope are
possible for China I think are going to be

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:54.640
suspect.
When it comes to how are we looking at China,

00:22:54.640 --> 00:23:02.020
oftentimes the stuff I've read in the past
showed China's development compared with Japan's

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:07.000
and they look at it that way. And I've always
thought that was the wrong analogy and that

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:14.740
the real comparison for us is going to be
this is going to be how the U.S. works with

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:19.419
China in this century. It's going to be a
lot more akin to what happened to the U.S.

00:23:19.419 --> 00:23:25.130
and the U.K. from 1830 to 1870.
The U.K. started off as the bigger economy

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:33.289
than the U.S. Over that forty years, our population
eclipsed them, our -- our regard in how we

00:23:33.289 --> 00:23:39.059
looked at business, how we encouraged business,
caused us to really outstrip them. We were

00:23:39.059 --> 00:23:43.630
focused on the -- even though they started
the industrial revolution, we really focused

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:49.559
on it and Americanized everything, especially
mass production. And we totally eclipsed their

00:23:49.559 --> 00:23:52.170
economy.
I think that same thing could happen in the

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:56.150
21st century because they already have almost
four times the number of people that we do.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:24:01.390
And as long as they focus on the right things
like they have historically, they could very

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:03.669
well get there.
So, I really think eighty-five years from

00:24:03.669 --> 00:24:07.890
now when people look back on this century,
the question is going to be how good a job

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:13.320
did our two countries do getting along and
how did we develop our relationship together?

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:18.730
And I think it's entirely possible for those
people who write all the books about confrontations

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:22.860
eleven out of fourteen times when countries
have been this big and this has happened -- I

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:26.059
really just don't think that has to be the
way this is.

00:24:26.059 --> 00:24:31.510
And I keep pointing to the U.K. that I just
described and how we look at them as our most

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:38.396
important allies -- and it was 200 years ago
that they burned down our White House.

00:24:38.396 --> 00:24:39.396
(LAUGHTER)
It was 150 years ago that we worked to keep

00:24:39.396 --> 00:24:44.620
them neutral in the Civil War. And somehow,
we've managed to make this work.

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:48.850
We need to be able to do the same thing with
China. We need to build our own competitiveness

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:52.390
agenda and we need to be doing a lot more
in the U.S. than we are today.

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:56.309
But at the same time, we have to do everything
we can to improve commercial and peaceful

00:24:56.309 --> 00:25:00.590
ties. That doesn't mean we just give up and
-- and let them do whatever they want, but

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:03.860
we need to always keep that in mind -- that
this is going to be an important relationship

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.950
to develop.
And it's one of the reasons I like to focus

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:09.940
on climate change and the discussions that
the president had because I think that's a

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:16.100
nice, peaceful way to accomplish a common
objective. We understand their needs to develop;

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:22.040
they also understand the needs to drive improvements
to prevent climate change and I think that's

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:26.980
a nice way to start building ties and making
sure this ends up being a peaceful economic

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.990
evolution rather than the way it could go
if -- if both countries aren't smart about

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:32.700
it.
ECONOMY: Great.

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:39.320
OK, Dr. Cai, you bring a unique perspective
to the panel. You grew up in China, you've

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:44.110
worked in China and you've worked with the
Chinese government. Give us your perspective

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:48.619
on Xi Jinping's reform plans. Where -- where
is it succeeding, where is it lagging, what's

00:25:48.619 --> 00:25:52.650
-- what do you see?
CAI: First of all, I'm very humbled to be

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:58.040
on this panel and in front of my -- my boss.
(LAUGHTER)

00:25:58.040 --> 00:26:06.929
So, you know, I -- I'm very optimistic. I
think so far for last two years, Xi Jinping

00:26:06.929 --> 00:26:15.150
definitely demonstrated he's the boss; he's
going to lead the China to overcome many other

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:19.679
countries have failed, which is called a middle
income trap.

00:26:19.679 --> 00:26:27.000
And when you look at all the issues -- corruption,
environmental degradation -- and, you know,

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:31.990
from my perspective, having the financial
sector -- a huge accumulation of risk in the

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:38.159
-- in the system. The fundamental issue is
really over the last many years China has

00:26:38.159 --> 00:26:47.679
been lucky in benefiting the global economic
progress -- you know, WTO, U.S. market -- and

00:26:47.679 --> 00:26:54.840
build up a system very hard to continue.
The business model is not going to work anymore

00:26:54.840 --> 00:27:01.760
in the future. But the key success factor
in the past business model is really the relationship

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.730
between the government and the -- and the
market.

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:09.179
The (inaudible) remember -- they're always
saying China is a different model, state of

00:27:09.179 --> 00:27:13.190
capitalism. By the way, you know, right now
the Chinese is the first one to say it's not

00:27:13.190 --> 00:27:19.049
working.
So, you look at the 18th party Congress and

00:27:19.049 --> 00:27:26.441
also the Third Plenary Session -- there's
a key phrase, which is, the center of the

00:27:26.441 --> 00:27:35.100
economic reform is allow the market to become
the decisive means to allocate resources.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:39.661
And when you look at the system, it's -- you
know, we look at the -- the cadre of Chinese

00:27:39.661 --> 00:27:45.429
Communist Party for last thirty years -- what
they know? They know to create GDP; and if

00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:47.809
you do a great job, 10 percent, 14 percent,
get promoted.

00:27:47.809 --> 00:27:55.200
And the -- everyone is like -- the mayor is
a CEO; the provincial governor is a CEO. And

00:27:55.200 --> 00:28:00.909
when you have -- the governments believe they
can -- they can do wonders. And that's where

00:28:00.909 --> 00:28:05.880
the corruption is; that's where the, you know,
kind of -- I would say damaging the environment

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:09.919
for my own promotion really at the expense
of the future generation.

00:28:09.919 --> 00:28:16.260
So, yes, there are all this political -- what
they call -- pretty harsh anti-corruption.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:23.160
I think in a country of this size with the
momentum, the leadership not only at the top

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:28.570
-- actually, it's throughout the country -- the
only I'll say means people know how to get

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:33.760
promoted, how to really get recognized is
creating new GDP; we need discipline. But

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:38.580
how will we get that discipline?
And by the way, another very important development

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:43.169
over last, you know, ten years, or twenty
years, a lot of wealth has been created. So,

00:28:43.169 --> 00:28:50.159
there are a lot of entrenched interests.
So, in a way, the tough measure of anti-corruption,

00:28:50.159 --> 00:28:55.080
why it's so supported by the public is because
people realize unless there's a fundamental

00:28:55.080 --> 00:29:00.360
governance reform, the country will get into
deep trouble.

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:05.990
And I think if you look at the team he has,
at least on the economic front, I think he

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:10.470
has the A team in China -- and certainly you
have Prime Minister Li. And equally important,

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:15.490
you know, Hank knows all of them and the governor
-- central bank governor, the minister of

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:21.130
finance and the -- what they call Council
of Economic Advisors. These are the people,

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:28.630
you know, I have been lucky to know; they
are fundamentally market-based thinker.

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:35.760
But they are also not what I would call ideology-driven;
they want to know in their next few years

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:42.559
what are the decisive actions they can take
to really stop this very rapid-growing moving

00:29:42.559 --> 00:29:48.809
train to really, you know, move the direction.
And if you are in China, Madeleine, you must

00:29:48.809 --> 00:29:53.649
have heard there's a phrase called the 'xin
chang tai' -- new norm. Everyone talking about

00:29:53.649 --> 00:29:58.510
new norm -- what does that mean?
New norm from the economics angle really are

00:29:58.510 --> 00:30:04.190
very simple. GDP is no longer the target.
If you read the newspaper, Shanghai government

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:10.549
for the first time no longer set any GDP target.
Number two is, no one's going to be rewarded

00:30:10.549 --> 00:30:14.900
if you are keep doing business not serving
the people, i.e., you are not really serving

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:19.710
-- government has to be a service provider,
not running a company.

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:23.980
And when you think of all the corruptions,
it's mainly because there's too much power

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:27.669
in the government, in the -- you know, in
the mayor, in the provincial hands. They can

00:30:27.669 --> 00:30:32.880
go to the bank, they can do all this -- have
a lot of ease -- very easy to make money.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:39.889
And they are also very focused on make sure
the overcapacity, a massive amount of literally

00:30:39.889 --> 00:30:47.049
-- I will say mid-level products -- has to
stop because it's creating huge risks for

00:30:47.049 --> 00:30:51.450
the financial system.
So, you know, you can do this in the very,

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:55.570
you know, Wall Street type -- Wall Street
Journal type of description. But there's one

00:30:55.570 --> 00:31:01.740
document I want to draw your attention -- I
think it's critical to demonstrate the leadership

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:06.580
determination. This is called the 'zhongyang
yihao wenjian' -- the 43rd document of the

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:11.701
State Council. It's called Strengthening the
-- and the -- "Strengthening the Regulation

00:31:11.701 --> 00:31:17.279
of Local Government Debt."
This is a fundamental piece of document. I

00:31:17.279 --> 00:31:22.270
think will fundamentally change the -- how
China can run between -- among different level

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:27.700
of government, between the government and
the business. Just give you a quick snapshot

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:31.010
on this -- I'd really encourage you to -- to
look at this.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:36.690
And by the way, the -- the genesis of this
document basically is saying, look, the local

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:42.620
government has been financing this GDP, you
know, through shadow banking, you -- you know,

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:48.779
these wealth management products, and through
variety of local government financing vehicles.

00:31:48.779 --> 00:31:53.809
Even the Chinese government at center they
even do not know how many. Based on the current

00:31:53.809 --> 00:31:59.600
numbers, about more than 7,000 debt-issuing
entities borrowing.

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:05.340
And they use this money to finance two things.
One is really a -- infrastructure. Every city

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:09.190
want to build the tallest building; everyone,
you know, doing something. And another thing

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:12.929
is trying to create a business because that's
creating tax for them.

00:32:12.929 --> 00:32:20.049
And because the government take away so much
liquidity from the system, that has created

00:32:20.049 --> 00:32:26.049
a huge problem for private sector to develop.
And the central government realized, Lou Jiwei

00:32:26.049 --> 00:32:30.290
in particular, and certainly the whole government
realized this is not sustainable.

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:36.669
What they did is basically they say, OK, by
the end of December 31st, 2014, we're going

00:32:36.669 --> 00:32:42.570
to call a staff of any local government ability
to raise debt. We're going to look at exactly

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:49.150
what we have, basically -- about how much
-- about 18 trillion RMB right now -- estimate;

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:57.200
and that's more than $2.5 trillion, all at
the local government level, spread at about

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:01.059
more than seven (inaudible), you know, different
variety of things.

00:33:01.059 --> 00:33:05.710
Basically, they are going to say from now
on, we're going to look at all this debt liability.

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:11.580
If the debt liability is for the public, i.e.,
infrastructure, the central government will

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:17.299
take over. From now on, local government cannot
issue any debt unless you go to the People's

00:33:17.299 --> 00:33:23.370
Congress of your local community.
So, you open up a transparency and the local

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:28.500
government right now -- they realize there's
no more money -- free money anymore. And when

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:35.360
you have your debt issued with approval, local
People's Congress, what do you get? You're

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:38.010
going to -- people are going to check what
are -- what are you doing -- where the money

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:42.100
has gone; what's your liability?
So, fundamentally, this has opened up the

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:48.309
transparency of government at a county, at
a city and a provincial level. This is, in

00:33:48.309 --> 00:33:54.440
my view, fundamental change in China. And
in my view, it's going to really define the

00:33:54.440 --> 00:34:00.940
border between the government and market.
And most interesting thing is going forward,

00:34:00.940 --> 00:34:05.570
the governments -- you can still issue debt,
by the way. But what you need to do is you

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:10.659
have to say what is that for. Is building
public, you know, public goods, or you are

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:15.649
doing for your recurrent expenditure? Not
that it would be allowed to issue for financing

00:34:15.649 --> 00:34:20.720
your so-called recurrent expenditure.
So, there are many, many fundamental change,

00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:25.560
only a -- you know, not really (inaudible)
in Wall Street Journal, in my view. I think

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:31.359
he's focused on the right thing. I think,
you know, I feel if he continues this path,

00:34:31.359 --> 00:34:35.790
the leadership will be able to move the China
into the right direction.

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:39.000
ECONOMY: Thank you.
So, one thing that I think we've sort of touched

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:43.540
on but haven't really addressed, and I -- I'd
like to get anybody's opinion on this before

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:49.500
we transition to discussion of the U.S.-China
bilateral relationship more explicitly -- is

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:55.300
whether the political tightening that is clearly
underway in China today, sort of the push

00:34:55.300 --> 00:35:01.150
through on the ideological front, through
think tanks in universities and sort of the

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:09.290
entire political system -- whether that is
-- contradicts Xi Jinping's economic objectives

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:15.180
-- his reform objectives, whether it's a sort
of short-term strategy designed to assist

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:19.390
in achieving those objectives -- what do you
see as the sort of linkage between what's

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:25.780
happening in the political sphere and the
economic sphere? Any takers?

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:32.040
ALBRIGHT: Well, I would say that there is
a result which I mentioned earlier, which

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:38.170
is if you want to have a consumer society,
if you want to figure out how to deal with

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:43.070
the local places, to what extent can you really
maintain centralized control?

00:35:43.070 --> 00:35:50.950
And an awful lot of what he is doing is trying
to kind of restore an ideology and a sense

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:57.410
of purpose and the -- the whole kind of drive
of who is China that he, in fact, has created

00:35:57.410 --> 00:36:00.430
a certain contradiction that he's going to
have to deal with.

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:06.430
And one of the issues in China is obviously
also the division between urban and rural,

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:12.580
Communist or not, between rich and poor. And
so, there are certain, I think, various things

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:17.440
that we feel in other societies that are going
to have to be dealt with in China and there

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:24.119
is truly a problem between overly centralized
government and a -- a one party system and

00:36:24.119 --> 00:36:27.920
the forces of the market. And at some point,
they're going to have to face that also.

00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:33.920
HUNTSMAN: I think the bottom line for Xi and
the regime is you cannot get -- and Dave Shambaugh's

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:38.119
written about this not so long ago -- you
can't get to the next level of economic development

00:36:38.119 --> 00:36:43.150
without fundamental legal and political reform,
period, because you cannot have a society

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:48.089
based on innovation or creativity or rule
of law or business adjudication; it's all

00:36:48.089 --> 00:36:54.369
ad hoc-ery and at the whims of bureaucrats.
So, and -- and I think Xi Jinping knows it.

00:36:54.369 --> 00:36:58.030
So, let's break it into phases. You've got
-- you've got the first phase which is consolidation

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:02.030
of power, which is what he's doing. And when
you're consolidating your power, I don't care

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:06.760
where -- where you are as a leader, I -- I
never did this as governor, though...

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:09.610
(LAUGHTER)
When -- when you consolidate power, you know,

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:14.710
you -- you cut off dissenting voices and opinions.
And so, the real test will be as we kind of

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:20.630
emerge beyond just the consolidation of power
anti-corruption campaign into the 19th party

00:37:20.630 --> 00:37:27.560
Congress, are we seeing more of the green
shoots as it relates to rule of law, maybe

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:32.570
even talk of civil society?
I know that's way premature but you know,

00:37:32.570 --> 00:37:36.630
I'm an optimist or I wouldn't be in this business.
I can't help but think that that ultimately

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:41.580
is where the population will take this over
time.

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:47.500
So, and I think Xi knows that without the
economic reforms bolstered by certain rule

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:53.349
of law and political reforms, China cannot
create the economy necessary -- necessary

00:37:53.349 --> 00:37:57.280
to provide its long-term stability, which
is his ultimate goal.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:01.930
COTE: And I -- I would agree on the -- I think
a lot of the things -- the changes that he's

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:07.100
making could be very good for the economy
because to get to the next level, I would

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:11.900
agree -- you have to build trust in your institutions.
People have to be able to trust those institutions

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:15.829
and we can all complain about them and some
of the decisions courts might make, at the

00:38:15.829 --> 00:38:18.530
same time.
But at the end of the day, if you've got some

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:23.369
faith, some trust that people are trying to
do the right thing in government, then that

00:38:23.369 --> 00:38:28.950
ends up being a good thing for development.
Anybody who's spent any time in China -- I

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:33.911
always found it kind of interesting when even
some people who might have spent some time

00:38:33.911 --> 00:38:38.650
there -- they would describe it as a totalitarian
system. So, tend to think of it as a monarchy

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:42.710
or a dictatorship and one guy decides what
happens and then everybody goes and does it.

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:47.510
One of the problems they're running into is
the fact that the provinces don't necessarily

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:53.380
listen to the central command. And I always
say if you want to get a sense for how well

00:38:53.380 --> 00:38:59.320
a people actually listen to the government,
get a police escort in Beijing. And as you

00:38:59.320 --> 00:39:03.650
start driving down the street trying to go
from the airport into the city, no one listens

00:39:03.650 --> 00:39:04.650
to the cops.
(LAUGHTER)

00:39:04.650 --> 00:39:08.050
They're yelling at them through the megaphone
and they're looking in their window, in their

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:09.050
mirror...
HUNTSMAN: Kind of like Washington.

00:39:09.050 --> 00:39:12.760
COTE: ...and they're not moving because they
want to get their first. And you say, OK,

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:16.760
other totalitarian systems I visited, people
listen to the cops.

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:20.610
(LAUGHTER)
So, it's not exactly the way we describe it.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:25.760
So, I can understand the need to consolidate
some of the power in order to be able to get

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:28.710
things done.
The thing I really worry about, getting back

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:35.270
to the trust in institutions, is, can he transition.
At some point, when the time is done, can

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:39.840
he pass it on to somebody else who can continue
the good work and recognize where it is they're

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:44.599
trying to go building faith in their institutions?
But overall, right now I think it's a positive.

00:39:44.599 --> 00:39:50.040
ECONOMY: OK, great.
So, I have a whole set of sort of U.S.-China

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:55.570
issue-specific questions like cybersecurity
and climate change but we have reached a moment

00:39:55.570 --> 00:40:00.810
where I think as chair, I really ought to
open the discussion to all of you. And we

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:06.010
have such an amazing audience that I'm sure
there are going to be a lot of really terrific

00:40:06.010 --> 00:40:10.940
questions.
So, let me now open the floor for questions

00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:18.000
from all of you. We have microphones so please
wait for a microphone to get to you. Please

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:24.050
identify yourself before you ask your question
and also just keep your questions and comments

00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:27.930
relatively concise.
Great, and I already see some hands. Why don't

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:36.440
we start right here?
QUESTION: Thank you. I'm Dan Yergin.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:42.140
Governor Huntsman, you diplomatically refer
to the risk of some tumult in U.S.-Chinese

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:47.540
relations between now and 2016. I wonder if
you all would like to riff on what that tumult

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:53.170
will be, and in particular, whether the South
China Sea controversies could be something

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:59.230
that could inadvertently derail the relationship.
HUNTSMAN: Thanks, Dan, for not asking a question

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:02.270
on energy.
(LAUGHTER)

00:41:02.270 --> 00:41:12.670
You know, having stood on the stage during
the last election cycle, the narrative of

00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:19.819
the U.S.-China relationship goes south real
fast because it's -- it's an easy villain

00:41:19.819 --> 00:41:24.760
for people to identify, to caricature and
to threaten.

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:30.540
So, at a time when we need a discussion about
a vision that bolsters the U.S. generally

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:35.180
-- the most important relationship in the
world, the one that gives context to our long-term

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:39.460
aspirations together -- I fear that we're
going to get a replay of what we had last

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:45.310
time, which is opportunistic discourse on
not what you're going to do with China, but

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:49.270
what you're going to do to China.
And so, this does a couple of things. It sets

00:41:49.270 --> 00:41:54.730
us back in terms of the progress we -- we
need to be making together, not on the issues

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:58.350
where we disagree, the -- there are plenty
of those and we have time to talk about -- but

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:03.520
issues where we do agree and how we can help
shape the world together because increasingly,

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:10.060
it must happen between the two of us as participants.
So -- so, it's lost opportunity on one side.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:17.520
Second, I think when you have the -- the -- the
totality of the candidates who begin to just

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:22.240
take the opportunity, there then is the punishment
period on the part of the Chinese when you

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:27.450
come up on the other side. So, whoever wins
the election is put kind of in the penalty

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:32.560
box, if you will. Six month cooling off period
-- is it a year cooling off period? You know

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:34.160
what I'm talking about.
(LAUGHTER)

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:38.280
In -- in -- in which -- in which the work
that really needs to get done shaped by a

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:42.339
vision discussed with the American people
during a campaign, which is what campaigns

00:42:42.339 --> 00:42:47.319
are supposed to be about, doesn't happen.
So, it's lost opportunity for the next three

00:42:47.319 --> 00:42:51.871
years.
And I just wonder about the patience on the

00:42:51.871 --> 00:42:58.030
other side where Xi Jinping is consolidating
power, he wants to strike some important agreements

00:42:58.030 --> 00:43:02.780
with the United States that will be part of
his legacy. And if we're not in the game to

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:06.710
play for the long term, which has always been
a tough thing for the United States, he'll

00:43:06.710 --> 00:43:10.890
go someplace else. And then we're really behind
in the game -- and that's what I fear could

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:17.440
result from the missed opportunity of 2016.
ECONOMY: OK, I think you had your hand up

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:25.150
next right there, in the middle?
QUESTION: Thank you. Don Clarke, GW Law School.

00:43:25.150 --> 00:43:34.090
I have a question for Dr. Cai. So, you mentioned
that Xi Jinping's goal is to replace the standard

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:39.310
of assessment for government officials. Instead
of, you know, how well do you produce stuff,

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:42.750
you know, how much GDP in your district, et
cetera, it's going to be how well you serve

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:45.730
the people.
So, I mean, this raises the question of how

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:52.190
you kind of operationalize that standard.
You know, if -- the best people to judge whether

00:43:52.190 --> 00:43:56.210
you're serving the people, you know, are of
course the people. But, you know, democratic

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:59.030
accountability downward is really off the
table.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:03.880
So, China is sort of stuck with the current
system, which is kind of bureaucratic ability

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:08.410
-- sorry, accountability upward. And some
things are easy to measure when you're doing

00:44:08.410 --> 00:44:11.940
that and some things are hard to measure.
So, things like GDP -- that's very easy to

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:17.600
measure and it's not easy to, you know, not
-- relatively not easy to, you know, tweak

00:44:17.600 --> 00:44:19.960
through corruption.
But if you're doing -- if you're trying to

00:44:19.960 --> 00:44:23.500
measure serving the people, I mean, that's
very hard to measure when you're trying to

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:28.630
do it through numbers that are reported to
your superior. So, how exactly could the standard

00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:35.450
really be operationalized?
CAI: It will be hard. And you talk about the

00:44:35.450 --> 00:44:40.000
risk, right? It's -- it's all the execution
risk, right, not being -- look at the leadership,

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:45.670
they talk about right things, they analyze
the whole issues. I mean, not only on the

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:51.579
GDP and the what to do with -- we talk about
intellectual property. You know, it's amazing

00:44:51.579 --> 00:44:56.910
that they are talking about unless we protect
intellectual property, no one's going to innovate;

00:44:56.910 --> 00:45:01.599
all these things.
I think there will be a, you know, from distance,

00:45:01.599 --> 00:45:07.820
look at -- China's truly a authoritarian -- whatever
we describe it. But there enough channel -- it's

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:13.500
not on political issue in terms of challenging
the party -- one party rule. But on a lot

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:18.370
of other things, you do have a pretty good
channel to say, hey, the traffic is bad; the

00:45:18.370 --> 00:45:23.320
water pollution -- the river runs black and
the schools are not doing well.

00:45:23.320 --> 00:45:28.569
So, I think -- let's start from basic health
care. There -- the Chinese have spent more

00:45:28.569 --> 00:45:33.641
than 3 trillion RMB to reform the health care
system; it's really not working out. The World

00:45:33.641 --> 00:45:37.859
Bank tried to be helpful. So, there are a
lot of those things -- basic needs.

00:45:37.859 --> 00:45:41.860
The middle class, right, now, they're looking
for a better quality of service. So, this

00:45:41.860 --> 00:45:48.250
is not as hard as if, you know, we have to
really go a long way to reflect because, you

00:45:48.250 --> 00:45:53.280
know, if you go to any Chinese hospital in
any big city or small city, it's not a very

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:58.369
comfortable experience. This has to be changed.
So, I would say there are a lot of visible

00:45:58.369 --> 00:46:02.369
program -- visible area -- they have to do
it. And there are a lot of other areas not

00:46:02.369 --> 00:46:07.500
that easy to -- to discern. But, you know,
let's deal with the low-hanging fruit first.

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:14.150
So, I think -- I'm not I'll say pessimistic
as if there's no channel feeding up what's

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:17.640
going on -- if progress has been made.
By the way, you know, one -- one of the biggest

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:23.310
issues -- 250 million migrant workers -- they
live in the city. Their children cannot go

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:30.280
to school and this is a big problem. Really,
is very, very -- you know, both make the people

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:33.130
who live in the city and not in the city very
uncomfortable.

00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:36.901
So, these are the issues I think -- if you
talk about many other issue we need to deal

00:46:36.901 --> 00:46:40.780
with, this probably the first thing -- basic
needs of -- of the people.

00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:44.180
ECONOMY: Great.
Yes? And if you could stand, please, when

00:46:44.180 --> 00:46:48.599
you ask your question? Oh, it's David.
QUESTION: David Sandalow, Columbia University.

00:46:48.599 --> 00:46:52.060
I'll ask the climate energy question. So,
could you place the climate agreement in the

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:56.010
context of the overall U.S.-China relationship?
What does it reflect about the relationship,

00:46:56.010 --> 00:47:00.870
what are the implications for the relationship
and what are the opportunities for U.S. business?

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:04.690
ECONOMY: Do you want to...
ALBRIGHT: Well, let me -- first of all, I

00:47:04.690 --> 00:47:13.369
do think that it was a -- a truly important
agreement between two individuals, frankly,

00:47:13.369 --> 00:47:18.839
very important to each of them for their agenda.
I think knowing, however, how complicated

00:47:18.839 --> 00:47:23.579
the whole climate story is and where it's
going in terms of the international -- the

00:47:23.579 --> 00:47:28.760
Paris meetings and all the various parts -- there's
the bilateral and then the multilateral part

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:33.369
of this that will be complicated -- but I
think it's a big step forward. Having been

00:47:33.369 --> 00:47:38.619
in negotiations previously where we couldn't
get anywhere, I think that it is a big step

00:47:38.619 --> 00:47:41.680
forward.
I think the issue generally, though, on issues

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:48.070
-- when issues are complicated between two
countries, you say they're multifaceted; this

00:47:48.070 --> 00:47:54.420
is definitely multifaceted relationship. And
-- and I do think that one plays off of another.

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:59.750
There will be questions to do with energy
and there will be questions to do with the

00:47:59.750 --> 00:48:05.030
South China Sea. And certainly those are the
kinds of issues that will come up during a

00:48:05.030 --> 00:48:08.130
campaign.
And, I do think that one of the other issues

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:14.330
that's out there in terms of domestic politics
is that one of the ways that you globalize

00:48:14.330 --> 00:48:21.760
a population is by letting loose the whole
concept of nationalism and that is partially

00:48:21.760 --> 00:48:27.280
what is going on in terms of the Chinese have
been put upon by everybody, including mostly

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:30.670
the Japanese.
But -- but basically, that nationalism is

00:48:30.670 --> 00:48:36.950
out there and will be brought forward if,
in fact, in some of these negotiations it

00:48:36.950 --> 00:48:42.360
is evident that China has had to make concessions
on issues. So, I think that that's where the

00:48:42.360 --> 00:48:47.410
domestic and foreign come together.
I do think from our perspective the climate

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:52.300
change is a big deal, but it is the first
step in a complex, multifaceted relationship.

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:56.050
ECONOMY: Do you want to say something? Yeah.
COTE: If I could, I -- for the same reasons

00:48:56.050 --> 00:49:01.720
I mentioned before, I liked the agreement
because I thought it was a -- a peaceful commercial

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:06.630
way to develop a -- a stronger relationship
between our two countries.

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:12.480
I also liked it because I thought there was
a recognition that China still needed to develop

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:16.580
and as they developed, emissions would grow,
but that they found a way to say "we'll peak

00:49:16.580 --> 00:49:21.470
in 2030" and then they'll start to go down.
So, I thought that was a nice indication that

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:25.010
the two countries could work together.
The potential for U.S. companies is quite

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:28.840
good because we have a lot of technologies
that could be helpful to them. If you take

00:49:28.840 --> 00:49:33.119
a look at the sulfur content of their fuel,
for example, removing all that sulfur before

00:49:33.119 --> 00:49:40.079
they put it into their cars; turbochargers
that can help them on miles per gallon; the

00:49:40.079 --> 00:49:46.540
new kinds of refrigerants that you can use
that have GWP below 1.0 -- there's a lot of

00:49:46.540 --> 00:49:50.190
stuff that's developed in the West.
The smart cities development -- they've got,

00:49:50.190 --> 00:49:55.201
say, another 300 million people that need
to move from the farms to the city. They're

00:49:55.201 --> 00:50:00.200
going to have a chance to develop cities that
make a lot more sense than the ones that we

00:50:00.200 --> 00:50:04.869
all developed in the West over centuries.
And U.S. technology can be quite important

00:50:04.869 --> 00:50:09.650
for them in thinking through how do you make
a smart city work. So, I think there's a lot

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:12.080
of play and opportunity for U.S. companies
here.

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:16.609
ECONOMY: Can I just ask -- do you see those
opportunities already for the U.S. environmental

00:50:16.609 --> 00:50:21.720
technologies? I remember a few years back,
the market seemed to be largely one where

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:25.290
the Chinese were saying, this is an area where
we ourselves can make a lot of progress, we're

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:28.400
not necessarily interested in opening up so
widely to U.S. companies.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:35.140
COTE: Yeah, I would have to put it on a continuum
and say it's shifted a bit. But there's still,

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:41.349
for example, low sulfur fuel technology -- something
we have today but they're trying strongly

00:50:41.349 --> 00:50:46.150
to develop it internally. The problem is it
never goes quite as fast because it's tougher

00:50:46.150 --> 00:50:49.910
than they -- than they think.
So, yes, that's still a problem, but I think

00:50:49.910 --> 00:50:53.601
you're going to start to see that shifting.
And we see it in smart city development, for

00:50:53.601 --> 00:50:58.000
example, where they -- they really want a
lot of perspective from the -- from the U.S.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:02.339
HUNTSMAN: I -- I think it's a really big deal,
David. We've worked on these issues before,

00:51:02.339 --> 00:51:06.200
we know the -- the complexity of it.
I was surprised, frankly -- although, when

00:51:06.200 --> 00:51:09.480
you look at it closely, you know, I can't
help but think, you know, that what the Chinese

00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:13.800
like to say, you know, they never forget anything
and we Americans never remember anything...

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:16.780
(LAUGHTER)
So -- so, who's going to be around in 2030?

00:51:16.780 --> 00:51:20.250
(LAUGHTER)
I don't know. And -- and how do we work it

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:25.270
out based on our domestic politics in America
where Hank is one of the few Republicans I

00:51:25.270 --> 00:51:29.130
know who can say the words climate change
without stuff being thrown at him.

00:51:29.130 --> 00:51:31.970
(LAUGHTER)
I don't know how we move the issue through

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:38.320
our system such that we have at least a policy
response, regardless of who the next president

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:45.350
is. It's important; the implications are enormous.
Peak levels by 2030 and effectively a renewable

00:51:45.350 --> 00:51:49.990
portfolio standard that goes with that, I
think -- I think China inexorably is moving

00:51:49.990 --> 00:51:56.579
there, not because they think it's -- it's
good policy as much as the political system

00:51:56.579 --> 00:52:01.240
is forcing them to -- the people of the country
are forcing them in that direction because

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:04.910
of the poor air quality. And so, we'll see
how that goes.

00:52:04.910 --> 00:52:06.930
CAI: OK, can I just comment on the...
ECONOMY: OK, quickly, quickly.

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.650
CAI: Yeah, there are two area -- I think one's
a nuclear -- they talk about a view -- nuclear

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:16.630
reactor. China has a plan to build fifty,
five-zero, nuclear reactors. And they view

00:52:16.630 --> 00:52:21.499
that as a key part of reducing emission.
Another very important area is agriculture

00:52:21.499 --> 00:52:26.791
-- smart agriculture, you -- has a lot of
things to offer. You know, the one example

00:52:26.791 --> 00:52:32.710
is Smithfield -- right now I think produce
a lot more pork in this country and export

00:52:32.710 --> 00:52:36.869
that to China. This has really spread into
many other area in agriculture.

00:52:36.869 --> 00:52:40.200
ECONOMY: Thank you.
OK, back there -- you've had your hand up

00:52:40.200 --> 00:52:43.040
for a bit.
QUESTION: Thank you. Richard Downie from Delphi

00:52:43.040 --> 00:52:47.240
Strategic Consulting. Thank you for a great
discussion; very interesting.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:54.280
A couple of you have obliquely mentioned cybersecurity
and I'll touch on it because I think cybersecurity

00:52:54.280 --> 00:53:01.240
is really one of those issues that stresses
this topic of competition versus cooperation.

00:53:01.240 --> 00:53:09.030
I mean, the Chinese have tremendous capabilities
in cyber and we've seen their ability to hack

00:53:09.030 --> 00:53:14.030
into our companies and they have made -- it's
intellectual property that's very similar

00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:20.740
to some of ours. You know, it's -- cybersecurity,
it's very difficult to -- to deter in this

00:53:20.740 --> 00:53:25.020
area. But, you know, intellectual property
is -- is an issue.

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:32.050
I wonder -- are any of you optimistic that
there is a framework that we can be cooperative

00:53:32.050 --> 00:53:34.130
in this -- this area, versus competition?
Thank you.

00:53:34.130 --> 00:53:36.430
ECONOMY: Let me ask you, Governor Huntsman,
because...

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:40.440
HUNTSMAN: Yeah, I -- I would agree that it's
-- it's an enormously important issue between

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:43.500
our two countries. It's -- it's right now
bad for both sides.

00:53:43.500 --> 00:53:49.670
So, I -- we did a study, I co-chaired it with
Admiral Denny Blair on particularly the intellectual

00:53:49.670 --> 00:53:53.890
property theft side, which is one that I think
we ought to think more about. You have the

00:53:53.890 --> 00:53:57.000
espionage side -- that's going to have to
be worked through channels that can draw red

00:53:57.000 --> 00:54:02.339
lines around whatever.
But the theft of intellectual property, which

00:54:02.339 --> 00:54:07.950
is robbing us of jobs, ideas and innovation,
is a real deal. And we assess the level of

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:12.500
injury to the United States to be about 300
billion bucks a year, which is a huge deal.

00:54:12.500 --> 00:54:17.430
So, it's bad for China because, you know,
why does China do -- why does anybody do it?

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:22.050
Because they're low-hanging opportunities.
You can take them; there are no rules of the

00:54:22.050 --> 00:54:26.460
road, there's no real punishment and you can
advance your technology base.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:31.560
The bad part of that is China's advancing
some of their technology base without the

00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:37.500
ecosystem or the infrastructure that is needed
to really give sustenance to those technologies.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:41.410
We built them here, we developed them, we
nurtured them in research universities, we

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:45.230
take them to the marketplace.
By China taking them, they don't have the

00:54:45.230 --> 00:54:51.130
support system that would really allow them
to -- to be viable over time, even if there

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:55.410
were equal rules of the road. So, that's bad
for China. It's bad for us just because of

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:59.860
the innovation that is stolen.
So, I'd say what do we do about it? It's a

00:54:59.860 --> 00:55:06.599
hard issue to talk about because we have no
real answers. It's the Wild West still.

00:55:06.599 --> 00:55:15.349
I look at the five PLA officers who were charged
in May of last year for ripping off secrets

00:55:15.349 --> 00:55:21.890
from U.S. Steel, Westinghouse -- maybe there
were one or two other companies. What's come

00:55:21.890 --> 00:55:30.980
of it? Nothing so far as I can tell. And I
remember in 2006 when, you know, unit 61398

00:55:30.980 --> 00:55:35.440
was stood up in Pudong right about when the
indigenous innovation drive started.

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:44.740
And so, there -- it's a real issue. And I
fear that the only answer long term -- and

00:55:44.740 --> 00:55:49.770
I wish we had something short term -- is the
new generation of entrepreneurs and innovators

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:54.440
putting pressure on their own government because
it begins to affect their ability to globalize

00:55:54.440 --> 00:55:59.920
and to take a good name into the marketplace.
And, you know, so I lived in Taiwan in 1987,

00:55:59.920 --> 00:56:06.090
1988 with the Hsinchu Industrial Park when
the DRAMs were being built. And they turned

00:56:06.090 --> 00:56:09.661
pretty quickly in terms of their own respect
for intellectual property rights because they

00:56:09.661 --> 00:56:13.619
had an indigenous movement that did so.
Finally, I'll just -- I'll just offer this

00:56:13.619 --> 00:56:18.930
-- we recommended in our report that you need
leverage in the game to get anybody's attention

00:56:18.930 --> 00:56:26.930
on this. So, why not look at access to the
marketplace, the U.S. marketplace, and make

00:56:26.930 --> 00:56:31.809
intellectual property theft through cyber
means or any other means part of the SEC review

00:56:31.809 --> 00:56:39.770
process if you want to take a company public,
part of the CFIUS review process, and part

00:56:39.770 --> 00:56:44.890
of financial transactions much like we do
when we sanction banks who launder money out

00:56:44.890 --> 00:56:49.410
of, say -- say, North Korea.
So, what has the administration not done?

00:56:49.410 --> 00:56:56.810
Well, nobody's looked real seriously about
how you respond based on market leverage.

00:56:56.810 --> 00:57:00.690
And then centralizing somebody who actually
owns the issue within the administration,

00:57:00.690 --> 00:57:04.310
which -- and unless you do that, put in the
hands of the national security advisor, I

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:08.230
don't know how you elevate it high enough
to where it really begins to take effect.

00:57:08.230 --> 00:57:12.810
ECONOMY: Yes?
COTE: As a recipient of numerous attacks from...

00:57:12.810 --> 00:57:17.089
(LAUGHTER)
All kinds of countries, I can say it's the

00:57:17.089 --> 00:57:21.819
-- to use an old Washington phrase, I guess,
it's the known unknown that has always scared

00:57:21.819 --> 00:57:23.640
me the most...
(LAUGHTER)

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:27.780
Because you -- no matter how much you tried
to protect yourself, you can't be sure that

00:57:27.780 --> 00:57:32.329
there isn't something already happening.
The issue that we run into is it's -- your

00:57:32.329 --> 00:57:38.960
border's no longer defined by a 200 mile limit.
So, the -- the -- if all of the sudden a hundred

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:43.460
people of another nationality landed on Long
Island, we'd know what to do. When they sit

00:57:43.460 --> 00:57:48.150
in another country attacking with their computer,
we don't know what to do.

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:52.420
And from my perspective, there needs to be
a treaty of some kind. We need to evolve to

00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:56.480
the point where we can -- and we can figure
out where an attack is coming from despite

00:57:56.480 --> 00:58:01.200
the point about servers attacking -- you know,
everybody can figure out where it's coming

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:05.450
from. We need some kind of treaty between
countries to say that you -- they're not going

00:58:05.450 --> 00:58:08.370
to do this.
And I agree on the whole point of innovation.

00:58:08.370 --> 00:58:12.720
If you're going to have an innovation culture,
you can't do it by stealing all the time.

00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:16.770
It doesn't -- it can help you get jumpstarted;
remember, we did the same with the U.K.

00:58:16.770 --> 00:58:18.750
(LAUGHTER)
But at some point, you have to stop and do

00:58:18.750 --> 00:58:22.329
it on your own if you're really going to evolve
your system.

00:58:22.329 --> 00:58:30.480
ECONOMY: Great. Yes?
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) Joanne Leedom-Ackerman

00:58:30.480 --> 00:58:32.430
from PEN International.
ECONOMY: Use the microphone -- sorry.

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:33.430
QUESTION: Joanne Leedom-Ackerman from PEN
International.

00:58:33.430 --> 00:58:38.910
I'll bring up the big word -- human rights
and freedom of expression. Obviously a society

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:44.020
doesn't develop if it cannot have free expression,
dissent, et cetera. What do you think the

00:58:44.020 --> 00:58:51.510
most productive way is to go forward as America
in our relationships with China with these

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:55.930
issues without, you know, hounding in a way
that's unproductive, but I don't think it's

00:58:55.930 --> 00:59:01.330
an issue that can be ignored?
ALBRIGHT: Well, I -- I think it's absolutely

00:59:01.330 --> 00:59:05.040
not an issue that can be ignored. And the
bottom line is -- is that when you do have

00:59:05.040 --> 00:59:11.079
negotiations, if you don't bring it up, then
you are not doing your job as an American

00:59:11.079 --> 00:59:14.569
representative.
And so, I think we do have to raise it; we

00:59:14.569 --> 00:59:19.450
have to always make clear -- and some of it
does have to do with cyber, frankly, in terms

00:59:19.450 --> 00:59:25.849
of under what circumstances do they want all
the -- the codes on people that are in banks;

00:59:25.849 --> 00:59:30.130
that's one of the stories that are coming
up now. And I think that we need to link those

00:59:30.130 --> 00:59:36.859
issues. And I think it's a mistake if we decide
that in this multifaceted relationship, that

00:59:36.859 --> 00:59:42.869
we will kind of be silent about it.
And -- and I think one of the issues here,

00:59:42.869 --> 00:59:47.150
and it goes a little bit to the question about
what happens at the bottom of the pyramid

00:59:47.150 --> 00:59:53.559
-- because people are trying to express themselves
with the problems that are there on water

00:59:53.559 --> 00:59:58.321
and food. And if there's some reason why we
had these meetings with the international

00:59:58.321 --> 01:00:03.069
department of the party, I think they were
trying to figure out how we relieve pressure

01:00:03.069 --> 01:00:08.619
at the bottom of the pyramid in terms of being
able to have people express their views.

01:00:08.619 --> 01:00:15.210
And so, I think we have to present the issue
of the capability of -- and it has to do with

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:20.440
innovation, et cetera, that a society is much
better off if it, in fact, allows some expression

01:00:20.440 --> 01:00:25.700
of views. So, we have to present it not just
as something that we care about but that ultimately

01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:32.089
that helps their society.
ECONOMY: Terrific. Yes?

01:00:32.089 --> 01:00:36.640
QUESTION: Thank you very much. Teresa Barger
from Cartica Capital.

01:00:36.640 --> 01:00:43.319
We live in an era where it's very tempting
to have competitive devaluations but we have

01:00:43.319 --> 01:00:49.920
not seen the Chinese engage in devaluing the
renminbi when everyone else is getting devalued

01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:53.690
all around them.
And there's a debate raging among the pundits

01:00:53.690 --> 01:00:59.030
-- some say, oh, well they are going to once
they get really terrified when their exports

01:00:59.030 --> 01:01:03.839
fall off. And there's another school of thought
that says, this is a great opportunity for

01:01:03.839 --> 01:01:10.190
the Chinese to bring everybody up the value
chain. And, in fact, a more expensive exchange

01:01:10.190 --> 01:01:16.740
rate is better for them to advance that agenda.
And so, I'm just wondering what the -- the

01:01:16.740 --> 01:01:19.760
-- since there are two views, what the views
of the panel are.

01:01:19.760 --> 01:01:25.500
ECONOMY: Dr. Cai, you want to take it?
CAI: Yeah, I think I agree with you. I think

01:01:25.500 --> 01:01:33.470
they are looking at this from much more automatic
economy reform perspective. And the -- yes,

01:01:33.470 --> 01:01:38.480
there have been a lot of export in the past
and the truth is they realize it's not worth

01:01:38.480 --> 01:01:43.359
it. They basically pollute the environment,
pollute the water -- exports for very marginal

01:01:43.359 --> 01:01:47.440
kind of a gain.
And another very important consideration is

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:52.299
really the RMB internationalization. If you
want to be a credible currency, you better

01:01:52.299 --> 01:01:58.310
make sure you -- you have a market-driven
kind of exchange rate or interest rate mechanism.

01:01:58.310 --> 01:02:05.960
So, I feel, you know, the second thought is
much more close to the decisionmakers' mind.

01:02:05.960 --> 01:02:10.031
When you look at what they are doing, it's
very much focused on domestic reform to move

01:02:10.031 --> 01:02:16.190
up the value chain. So, I think the chance
of competitive devaluation conducted by the

01:02:16.190 --> 01:02:26.800
central bank is very, very small.
ECONOMY: Great. Yes, right there, in the center?

01:02:26.800 --> 01:02:28.970
QUESTION: Thanks, hi. Marty Weiss with the
Congressional Research Service.

01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:33.260
My question is about the bilateral relationship
in the context of the IMF, the World Bank

01:02:33.260 --> 01:02:37.280
and the multilaterals. As, you know, quota
reform at the IMF to increase China's share

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:42.020
isn't going anywhere, China at the same time
is playing a much more sort of role establishing

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:48.150
the Asian -- the Infrastructure Investment
Bank, and other type of entities, as well.

01:02:48.150 --> 01:02:52.369
What should the right U.S. response be? How
should the U.S. balance its interest in protecting

01:02:52.369 --> 01:02:56.799
our safeguards, our priorities? The -- the
World Bank and the Asian Development Bank

01:02:56.799 --> 01:03:01.579
is, you know, in their role while also trying
to shape China's choices as, you know, the

01:03:01.579 --> 01:03:05.630
inevitable occurs -- as they create these
types -- these new entities?

01:03:05.630 --> 01:03:07.980
ECONOMY: Governor Huntsman, you want to take
that?

01:03:07.980 --> 01:03:13.740
HUNTSMAN: Oh, I think -- I think China is
very adverse to U.S.-centric institutions.

01:03:13.740 --> 01:03:16.839
I think they'd like to build some of their
own. I think the Infrastructure Bank is the

01:03:16.839 --> 01:03:23.310
latest example -- maybe even a trial balloon
in trying to do just that, to see how the

01:03:23.310 --> 01:03:28.579
region would respond. And of course Korea,
Japan, and Australia didn't respond favorably.

01:03:28.579 --> 01:03:34.240
I -- I think we took the wrong approach on
that. I think instead of threatening, trying

01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:41.010
to create two separate worlds, why not shape
it? Why not help to shape a common destiny?

01:03:41.010 --> 01:03:46.490
I think that was a fundamental mistake.
But we'll see where institution building goes.

01:03:46.490 --> 01:03:54.030
I think we're just on the front end of some
other attempts by -- by China to create regional

01:03:54.030 --> 01:04:00.369
institutions, maybe headed toward global institutions,
that help to balance America's role in the

01:04:00.369 --> 01:04:02.490
world. I think fundamentally, that's -- that's
what they seek.

01:04:02.490 --> 01:04:05.650
CAI: I have a -- because I work in the World
Bank?

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:07.240
ECONOMY: Sure.
(LAUGHTER)

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:16.250
CAI: I think China want to -- to play ball.
And, you know, this -- this -- the Chinese,

01:04:16.250 --> 01:04:20.770
when you think about it, they've gone through
phases. I think the most recent indication

01:04:20.770 --> 01:04:25.880
is that they feel, you know, certainly they
can do another bank. But they cannot substitute

01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:28.940
the quote-unquote reputation of the World
Bank.

01:04:28.940 --> 01:04:34.720
But I would say just one thing I found always
kind of I will say frustrating -- and every

01:04:34.720 --> 01:04:40.839
time they talk about China, they're always,
you know, we lecture them -- our safeguard.

01:04:40.839 --> 01:04:44.750
My own view is working in the World Bank,
I think we -- in the World Bank, we can do

01:04:44.750 --> 01:04:50.589
so much more for emerging market. If we just
relax in terms of how we solve problem rather

01:04:50.589 --> 01:04:55.020
than insisting certain things, sometimes really
in my view is not practical.

01:04:55.020 --> 01:05:00.040
That's why for the longest time I feel -- you
know, we talk about Ebola. I just with Dr.

01:05:00.040 --> 01:05:04.230
Kim went to Ebola countries. I -- is a -- you
know, they're a lot of work to do. The truth

01:05:04.230 --> 01:05:08.789
is the World Bank -- we have been operating
in many countries for so long; we need to

01:05:08.789 --> 01:05:13.490
see faster buildup on infrastructure. We need
to do a lot of things.

01:05:13.490 --> 01:05:18.549
I think I agree with the ambassador here.
If we find a way to work with the Chinese

01:05:18.549 --> 01:05:22.789
and they have so much more capital to deploy,
they're looking for some kind of a partnership

01:05:22.789 --> 01:05:27.630
because after all this trial -- if three years
ago, if you said let's be partners, I don't

01:05:27.630 --> 01:05:31.000
think the Chinese -- hey, we can do it on
our own. But right now, they realize it's

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:34.280
not that fun; it's very difficult to do things
on their own.

01:05:34.280 --> 01:05:38.360
So, I think there's opportunity here. I'm
not too sure -- call that mistake -- you know,

01:05:38.360 --> 01:05:42.721
the partnership can be built, even starting
from now. So, I feel, you know, at least from

01:05:42.721 --> 01:05:47.079
the World Bank perspective, we're have been
a lot of positive inclination trying to be

01:05:47.079 --> 01:05:51.630
a partner with the new bank if that bank ever
going to be in operation.

01:05:51.630 --> 01:05:58.760
ECONOMY: Thanks. OK, right here, yeah.
QUESTION: Thank you. Jack Goldstone, Woodrow

01:05:58.760 --> 01:06:03.869
Wilson Center in George Mason University.
I have a question about the university sector.

01:06:03.869 --> 01:06:09.210
They just announced a crackdown on deviant
thinking for universities. Now, my question

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:15.250
is does the Chinese leadership really understand
what's necessary for a knowledge economy and

01:06:15.250 --> 01:06:18.150
innovation?
They have set targets for patents and generated

01:06:18.150 --> 01:06:23.260
thousands of incremental, insignificant patents.
They've set targets for faculty to publish

01:06:23.260 --> 01:06:29.369
in journals and unleashed a flood of plagiarism
and massive duplication of submissions to

01:06:29.369 --> 01:06:34.079
journals. They've encouraged faculty to give
lectures; they've turned them into junkets.

01:06:34.079 --> 01:06:38.940
So, they understand setting targets. They
don't seem to me to understand that a knowledge

01:06:38.940 --> 01:06:44.450
economy really requires freedom of expression,
freedom of inquiry and open thought. Do you

01:06:44.450 --> 01:06:49.339
think they get that and are just sitting on
it or do they really have to work to understand

01:06:49.339 --> 01:06:50.339
this?
(LAUGHTER)

01:06:50.339 --> 01:06:51.520
HUNTSMAN: Dr. Cai...
ECONOMY: Right.

01:06:51.520 --> 01:06:55.069
COTE: If we eliminate deviant...
HUNTSMAN: That's tailor-made for you.

01:06:55.069 --> 01:06:59.309
CAI: Here -- here -- here is the issue, right?
At the beginning, I mentioned it -- the government

01:06:59.309 --> 01:07:03.890
for the last thirty years -- the government
believes it can do wonders. Anything government

01:07:03.890 --> 01:07:08.640
want to do can be achieved. But this is one
area that's not going to be that easy.

01:07:08.640 --> 01:07:14.900
I would say there's a -- more and more recognition.
Right now, you know, the university is almost

01:07:14.900 --> 01:07:18.700
like a bureaucracy right now, as exactly what
you said.

01:07:18.700 --> 01:07:23.820
So, while this can continue and there are
a lot of new interesting development, I don't

01:07:23.820 --> 01:07:25.990
know if you -- I always follow this latest
development.

01:07:25.990 --> 01:07:32.830
Jack Ma just set up a new university. Who
-- who is on his board? The (inaudible) -- Hank

01:07:32.830 --> 01:07:38.940
would know -- Dr. Chen, my good friend; he's
the director -- I mean the dean of the school

01:07:38.940 --> 01:07:46.330
of economic management in the Beijing University.
There's some U.S.-educated, I'll say leaders

01:07:46.330 --> 01:07:48.920
-- real education leaders, and they are getting
on the board.

01:07:48.920 --> 01:07:54.380
So, there -- I think -- look, it's -- it's
a massive system; 7 million graduates each

01:07:54.380 --> 01:07:58.080
year. I mean, it's -- it's not going to be
easy. But you start to see some interesting

01:07:58.080 --> 01:08:02.600
examples.
I think over time, I hope we will get to,

01:08:02.600 --> 01:08:08.329
you know, what's the bureaucratization -- whatever
they call it -- that's the term they call

01:08:08.329 --> 01:08:11.470
it right now, yeah.
ALBRIGHT: There's one other side to this that

01:08:11.470 --> 01:08:17.210
I think is worth looking at. There are American
universities that do not wish to deal with

01:08:17.210 --> 01:08:20.980
Chinese universities because of what you're
talking about.

01:08:20.980 --> 01:08:26.670
And I think that what happens then when there
aren't exchange programs is we are basically

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:31.819
undercutting our own issues by not allowing
the people-to-people exchanges and having

01:08:31.819 --> 01:08:36.299
the kind of issues that students can talk
about.

01:08:36.299 --> 01:08:41.210
And so, that -- I definitely think there's
a problem in terms of what they're doing.

01:08:41.210 --> 01:08:47.690
But I hope that it does mean that those that
I teach -- those of us that are involved in

01:08:47.690 --> 01:08:51.500
education and people-to-people and student
exchanges -- don't decide we don't want anything

01:08:51.500 --> 01:08:54.759
to do with them.
COTE: I'd say at the same time -- I have to

01:08:54.759 --> 01:08:59.279
add this -- given I guess that I'm an honorary
professor at Beihang University...

01:08:59.279 --> 01:09:03.089
(LAUGHTER)
And just outside of Beijing. And I -- what

01:09:03.089 --> 01:09:07.960
I find absolutely fascinating about their
commitment to education -- so, not that the

01:09:07.960 --> 01:09:11.960
deviant thinking is such a great thing to
I guess encourage or discourage; I wonder

01:09:11.960 --> 01:09:14.420
how many kids we'd have left in school if
we took that out.

01:09:14.420 --> 01:09:16.859
(LAUGHTER)
But at the end of the day, they still have

01:09:16.859 --> 01:09:21.089
this commitment to math and science education
that is unbelievably impressive. And when

01:09:21.089 --> 01:09:26.929
I go there to talk to them, which is about
once a year, everything's in English. So,

01:09:26.929 --> 01:09:30.500
all the kids are taught in English, all the
math and science classes are in English; they're

01:09:30.500 --> 01:09:35.009
learning everything in English from the first
day they get there and they have thousands

01:09:35.009 --> 01:09:39.299
of kids enrolled in this aeronautical university.
How many kids do you think we'd get in the

01:09:39.299 --> 01:09:43.609
U.S. if we said we're going to have an aeronautical
university and you're going to learn everything

01:09:43.609 --> 01:09:49.100
in Mandarin? How many kids would go? Yet they
have thousands who want to do this.

01:09:49.100 --> 01:09:52.719
And I wouldn't lose sight of what their university
system can produce.

01:09:52.719 --> 01:09:57.929
HUNTSMAN: I would just say I think one of
their longer-term aspirations is to create

01:09:57.929 --> 01:10:02.660
a research university. I think they envy our
research university model.

01:10:02.660 --> 01:10:10.270
Now, why do I say that? For all kinds of reasons,
not least of all the -- the cyber intrusions

01:10:10.270 --> 01:10:15.970
into certain research universities here that
are focused on translational research. They

01:10:15.970 --> 01:10:19.260
don't do it; we do, better than anybody in
the world. And I think that's where they'd

01:10:19.260 --> 01:10:23.330
like to be in the future.
They'd love to incubate, nurture, innovate

01:10:23.330 --> 01:10:27.239
and translate it into the marketplace. I think
that's something they -- they really envy

01:10:27.239 --> 01:10:31.310
about our system. And I do believe that on
the educational side, they will move more

01:10:31.310 --> 01:10:35.620
and more in that direction along with other
educational innovations that I think are going

01:10:35.620 --> 01:10:39.060
to be quite profound in the country.
COTE: I -- I agree with you.

01:10:39.060 --> 01:10:41.219
CAI: And if I may add just one point here...
(LAUGHTER)

01:10:41.219 --> 01:10:42.219
ECONOMY: OK...
CAI: Very interesting. I...

01:10:42.219 --> 01:10:44.699
ECONOMY: We're going to lose...
CAI: In the past, I never -- you know, always

01:10:44.699 --> 01:10:51.670
-- can China innovate. If you go to Shenzhen
nowadays, it's amazing. I mean, when I was

01:10:51.670 --> 01:10:55.449
a banker, I always, oh, really good there
-- any -- you know, (inaudible). This guy

01:10:55.449 --> 01:11:02.450
was educated in China, right? We called Beijing
a German institute -- 5,000 young scientists.

01:11:02.450 --> 01:11:07.140
I think China is on its way to become a leader
in that -- bioengineering, bioscience. There

01:11:07.140 --> 01:11:13.219
are massive amounts of innovation, I -- technology;
not necessarily in the other area, but at

01:11:13.219 --> 01:11:17.140
least in technology, I am very hopeful. I
mean, I'm positively surprised.

01:11:17.140 --> 01:11:20.070
HUNTSMAN: But it's certainly appropriate that
Jack Ma, a friend...

01:11:20.070 --> 01:11:22.880
ALBRIGHT: Yeah.
HUNTSMAN: ...who failed the entry exam three

01:11:22.880 --> 01:11:27.900
times to get into college is leading the efforts
toward reform. I think that's awesome.

01:11:27.900 --> 01:11:31.940
(LAUGHTER)
ECONOMY: OK, this is unfortunately going to

01:11:31.940 --> 01:11:37.090
have to be our last question. Go ahead, sir.
QUESTION: Would you say a few words about

01:11:37.090 --> 01:11:42.860
the Russia-China entente and whether -- what
-- how China views that? Do they see it as

01:11:42.860 --> 01:11:46.211
an opportunity to make Russia into a vassal
state? Do they see Russia as a...

01:11:46.211 --> 01:11:50.460
(LAUGHTER)
...as a -- as a -- as a tributary? Do they

01:11:50.460 --> 01:11:54.740
see it as an equal? I don't think so. But
I'd love to have some comments on that. Thank

01:11:54.740 --> 01:11:57.820
you.
ALBRIGHT: Well, I think they are finding it

01:11:57.820 --> 01:12:03.340
very useful and the Russians are certainly
going along with it. You have the expert sitting

01:12:03.340 --> 01:12:08.710
next to you in terms of building pipelines
and trying to figure out how to deal with

01:12:08.710 --> 01:12:15.320
China's energy issues.
I do think that there is a reconstitution

01:12:15.320 --> 01:12:21.750
terms -- kind of a who's in power where, who
are the global powers. And we have seen periods

01:12:21.750 --> 01:12:26.370
of history where the Chinese-Russian relationship
has been close and others where they have

01:12:26.370 --> 01:12:31.199
gone opposite ways.
But I think it is a testing period and I imagine

01:12:31.199 --> 01:12:35.980
that at the moment, frankly, China is in a
much stronger position in making deals than

01:12:35.980 --> 01:12:43.210
the Russians are, and trying to figure out
what they can get out of it and how important

01:12:43.210 --> 01:12:48.460
it is to them. I think it's fascinating to
watch and I bet you that they also talk about

01:12:48.460 --> 01:12:52.670
how we play in that game.
ECONOMY: And how do you think they talk about

01:12:52.670 --> 01:12:53.670
that -- about how...
(LAUGHTER)

01:12:53.670 --> 01:12:58.270
ALBRIGHT: Well, I -- I think that they can
often find that they have more in common with

01:12:58.270 --> 01:13:03.920
each other than with us, certainly in terms
of length of borders and various issues. But

01:13:03.920 --> 01:13:09.409
I also think that the Chinese are smart enough
to know that their most important relationship

01:13:09.409 --> 01:13:14.460
is with us, just as I believe that our most
important relationship in the 21st century

01:13:14.460 --> 01:13:17.780
is with them.
ECONOMY: OK, I think that's an excellent note

01:13:17.780 --> 01:13:23.940
on which to end our discussion this evening.
Let me please ask all of you to join me in

01:13:23.940 --> 01:13:25.010
thanking our wonderful panelists.
(APPLAUSE)

